From cbccf3ff1df9c1073c01049a581b6de07e202a63 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gerardo Amaya <gerardo@example.com>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 19:10:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat: deployment setup for Railway, Neon and Vercel

One worker, deliberately. The per-IP limiter keeps its count in process
memory, so four workers would each hold their own and the real ceiling would
be four times the configured one, with nothing failing and nothing warning.
The choice not to add Redis forces a single process, and that is pinned in
start.sh with the reason next to it rather than left to whatever the platform
defaults to. If it ever needs to scale, the order is shared storage first,
workers second; the other way round the limit stops limiting silently.

Migrations run as a pre-deploy command rather than in the container's
entrypoint. With two replicas, migrating on boot has both running Alembic
against the same database at once, and a migration that fails inside the
entrypoint leaves the app serving half-migrated instead of stopping the
deploy.

Data gets copied from local rather than re-downloaded. The README's figures --
5,652 active places, 5.9% discarded, a 1.45 detour factor over 812 pairs --
describe one specific snapshot of the catalogue. Pulling from Overpass again
would produce a different one, since OpenStreetMap changes daily, and the
README would quietly start describing something that no longer exists.

The dump includes travel_edges on purpose: those are OpenRouteService
requests already paid for out of a fifty-a-day quota, and carrying them means
the demo starts with a warm cache in every zone that has been queried.

The start script goes through a shell because the exec form of CMD does not
expand variables and Railway injects the port.
